ter than the coldest (about 0.35 vs 0.25 °C per decade). There is a strong, well-established El Niño effect on temperature: after El Niño conditions in the preceding fall, Mar–May tends to run warmer, and El Niño accounts for about 10% of the year-to-year ups and downs. With this year's strong El Niño, that push is about as large as a normal year-to-year swing, roughly as much warmth as 16 years of the long-term trend. Without El Niño, a Mar–May this warm would come about once every 6 years. Mar–May rainfall has fallen by about 5% per decade, with no clear El Niño link. Relative humidity has fallen by about 0.9 points per decade, roughly 7 points since 1951; because warmer air holds more moisture, this does not necessarily mean the air is drier. For Mar–May 2027, a warmer-than-normal season is likely (about 95% chance), about 1.3 °C above the 1991–2020 average, mostly because of the long-term warming trend rather than El Niño. The El Niño expected for Sep–Nov 2026 is stronger than any in the record used here, so the outlook extends the historical relationship beyond what has been observed. This outlook is based on the El Niño strength expected for Sep–Nov 2026 and will be updated when it is observed in early December.","variables":{"temperature":{"normal_1991_2020":17.2637,"trend_per_decade":0.351,"trend_p":0.0,"trend_total_since_1951":2.6323,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.2225,"enso_p":0.0022,"confidence":"Strong","survives_fdr":true,"fraction_explained":0.1232,"intrinsic_sd":0.5564,"effect_this_season":0.563,"effect_ratio":1.012,"size":"Dominant","size_typical_el_nino":"Substantial","once_every_n_years_without_el_nino":6,"expected_anomaly_2027":1.2577,"forecast_mean":18.5215,"forecast_sd":0.6052,"p_below":0.0043,"p_near":0.0625,"p_above":0.9332,"forecast_words":"Likely (93%): a warmer-than-normal season","within_record":false,"trend_part_2027":0.6947,"roni_upper_within_record":false,"years_of_warming_equivalent":16.0426,"percentiles":{"p05":{"trend_per_decade":0.2699,"t